# Blog Generator — use the LLM as a writing assistant
#
# This example shows a non-code use case:
#   - Generate blog posts from a topic
#   - Reusable prompt templates with functions (@use)
#   - Task arguments for customization
#
# Try it:
#   justprompt --list                          # see available tasks
#   justprompt --dry-run draft "async Python"  # preview
#   justprompt draft "Docker networking"       # generate a blog post

llm claude [model=sonnet]

# Reusable writing style guide
fn writing-style:
    Write in a clear, conversational tone.
    Use short paragraphs. Avoid jargon unless you explain it.
    Include practical code examples where relevant.
    Target audience: intermediate developers.

# Generate a blog post draft
task draft(topic):
    @use writing-style
    Write a technical blog post about: {{topic}}
    Include:
    - A catchy title
    - An introduction that hooks the reader
    - 3-4 main sections with subheadings
    - Code examples where appropriate
    - A conclusion with key takeaways
    Format as Markdown.

# Summarize an existing article
task summarize:
    !cat article.md 2>/dev/null || echo "No article.md found — create one first."
    @use writing-style
    Summarize the article above into 3-5 bullet points.
    Each bullet should capture one key idea.

# Generate a tweet thread from a blog post
task tweet-thread:
    !cat article.md 2>/dev/null || echo "No article.md found — create one first."
    Turn the article above into a Twitter/X thread of 5-8 tweets.
    First tweet should hook the reader.
    Last tweet should have a call to action.
    Use plain text, no markdown.
